\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

\looseness=-1 We identify conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO) as a systematic failure mode of temporal conditioning in imagination-based actor-critic training. Trained $\dt$-embeddings with multi-rate data exposure reliably induce value overestimation while frozen embeddings never do, revealing co-adaptation between the temporal embedding and the world model as the causal factor. A four-level intervention hierarchy yields two key insights: gradient magnitude does not predict causal importance, and dual critics converge in pure imagination, so Double Q pessimism is structurally ineffective in any system with shared imagined rollouts. For practitioners, we recommend: (1)~validate with frozen-embedding controls before deploying learned embeddings; (2)~monitor NVE as an early warning; (3)~prefer MPC-style re-planning for variable-rate execution. Code and configurations will be released upon acceptance.

