\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{CIVO mechanism.}
\looseness=-1 CIVO propagates through multiple pathways: Walker Walk develops a $1.63\times$ reward prediction gap while Cheetah Run shows none ($1.00\times$) despite higher severity ($8.27\times$ NVE). In Cheetah, observation fidelity reverses ($0.93\times$; $\dt{=}2$ predictions more accurate) while embedding geometry separates comparably (cosine ${\leq}{-}0.84$), suggesting CIVO propagates through latent-space dynamics rather than reward prediction; a random-policy decomposition attributes ${\approx}36\%$ of overestimation to dynamics bias and ${\approx}64\%$ to actor exploitation (Appendix~\ref{sec:civo_mechanism}). The mechanism is analogous to delusional bias \citep{lu2018nondelusional} but model-induced; unlike MPC, imagination-based actor-critic bootstraps value targets entirely within the learned model without real-data correction (Appendix~\ref{sec:mpc_immune}). Frozen embeddings participate identically in the forward pass yet never trigger CIVO (0/16 controls vs.\ 5/7 trained, $p < 0.001$; Table~\ref{tab:causal_ablation}): the vulnerability lies in what the embedding \emph{learns}, not in conditioning architecture.

\vspace{-2pt}
\paragraph{Why interventions fail; practical implications.}
\label{sec:detector}
\looseness=-1 CIVO is an emergent property of co-adapting embeddings, not a single-pathway defect: SGS fails from pathway redundancy; RAVE fails as the EMA anchor tracks biased values; TCN shows binary behavior suggesting a phase transition. At the architecture level, dual critics converge in pure imagination ($\rho > 0.999$, $n{=}7$): shared imagined rollouts produce near-identical training data for both critics, so Double Q pessimism---effective in model-free settings---structurally vanishes. No intervention transfers across environments at the same hyperparameters: TCN's effective dose differs $5\times$ between Walker ($\sigma{=}0.2$) and Cheetah ($\sigma{=}1.0$, with policy erosion to $15\%$ baseline); wide dual-critic prevents 3/3 Walker but 0/1 Cheetah. Even the strongest result---wide dual-critic (NVE $\leq 1.4\times$, 3/4 seeds)---remains environment-specific. The structural vulnerability is the closed-loop nature of imagination: the same model parameters generate both the critic's training distribution and its bootstrap targets, so bias in $\dt{>}1$ predictions compounds without external correction---a self-reinforcing cycle that single-pathway interventions cannot break. These findings generalize beyond DreamerV3: embedding co-adaptation may interact pathologically with value estimation in any imagination-based actor-critic that bootstraps within a learned model.

\vspace{-2pt}
\paragraph{Environment complexity boundary.}
\looseness=-1 Cross-environment analysis across six DMC tasks spanning 1--21 DoF reveals that CIVO occupies a complexity Goldilocks zone: 1-DoF (Cartpole, 0/3) and 4-DoF (Hopper, 0/1) never trigger onset, while 12-DoF (Quadruped) and 21-DoF (Humanoid) baselines exhibit intrinsic value overestimation (NVE $4.7\times$ and $12.97\times$ without the gate mechanism) that precludes CIVO isolation. Onset occurs exclusively at 6 DoF (Walker 60\%, Cheetah 75\%; Tables~\ref{tab:cross_env_civo} and~\ref{tab:dof_boundary}), suggesting CIVO requires sufficient dynamic complexity for temporal embeddings to develop exploitable value bias, yet a stable baseline where gate-induced perturbation dominates. Practitioners deploying temporal conditioning should monitor NVE particularly in intermediate-complexity systems where baseline value estimation is stable but dynamics are complex enough for embedding co-adaptation. For transformer-based world models \citep{micheli2023iris}, whether this boundary shifts remains open.

